1
00:03:57,272 --> 00:04:03,272
Get ready to jam.

2
00:07:54,545 --> 00:08:00,545
Space Jam is a comedy, animation, family film directed by Joe Pytka.

3
00:11:51,818 --> 00:11:57,818
With their freedom on the line, the Looney Tunes seek the help of NBA superstar
Michael Jordan to win a basketball game against a team of moronic aliens.

4
00:15:49,090 --> 00:15:55,090
Michael Jordan, played by Michael Jordan.

5
00:19:46,363 --> 00:19:52,363
Stan Podolak, played by Wayne Knight.

6
00:23:43,636 --> 00:23:49,636
Juanita Jordan, played by Theresa Randle.

7
00:27:40,909 --> 00:27:46,909
Jeffery Jordan, played by Manner Washington.

8
00:31:38,181 --> 00:31:44,181
Marcus Jordan, played by Eric Gordon.

9
00:35:35,454 --> 00:35:41,454
Jasmine Jordan, played by Penny Bae Bridges.

10
00:39:32,727 --> 00:39:38,727
Space Jam is a 1996 American live-action animated sports comedy film directed by Joe Pytka and
written by Leo Benvenuti, Steve Rudnick, Timothy Harris, and Herschel Weingrod.

11
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:36,000
The first film produced by Warner Bros.

12
00:47:27,272 --> 00:47:33,272
Feature Animation, it stars basketball player Michael Jordan as himself; the live-action cast also includes
Wayne Knight and Theresa Randle while Billy West and Danny DeVito headline the voice cast.

13
00:51:24,545 --> 00:51:30,545
The film follows Jordan as he is brought out of retirement by the Looney Tunes characters to help them win a
basketball match against invading aliens intent on enslaving them as amusement park attractions.

14
00:55:21,818 --> 00:55:27,818
Space Jam premiered in Los Angeles on November 10, 1996, and was released
theatrically in the United States on November 15, by Warner Bros.

15
00:59:19,090 --> 00:59:25,090
under its Family Entertainment label.

16
01:03:16,363 --> 01:03:22,363
The film received mixed reviews from critics, who were divided over its premise of combining Jordan and his profession with the
Looney Tunes characters, while the technical achievements of its intertwining of live-action and animation were praised.

17
01:07:13,636 --> 01:07:19,636
It was a commercial success, grossing $250.2 million worldwide against an $80 million budget, becoming the
highest-grossing basketball film of all time until 2022, and the tenth-highest-grossing film of 1996.

18
01:11:10,909 --> 01:11:16,909
A standalone sequel, Space Jam: A New Legacy, was released
in 2021, with LeBron James in the lead role.

19
01:15:08,181 --> 01:15:14,181
The sequel received generally negative reviews and failed to
match the commercial success of the first film.

20
01:19:05,454 --> 01:19:11,454
The film explores themes of sports, cartoon,
villain, basketball, slapstick comedy.

21
01:23:02,727 --> 01:23:08,727
The tone is lighthearted and thought_provoking.
